{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 38, :comments ["<b>And its tongs and its firepans.</b> It is known that counsel and thought are tongs (מלקחים) for the Torah, in the manner of what is written (Proverbs 1:5): ״ונבון תחבולות יקנה״ — 'and one who understands devices shall acquire.' And thought is in the heart, and the heart he likened to a firepan (מחתה), for just as the firepan is a vessel from which one takes fire to kindle the lamps, so the heart is the vessel of the intellect, which a person uses in the Torah. And its weight was a talent of gold, no less and no more, on account of the principle that one may not add to the Torah and may not subtract from it, in the manner of what is said (Deuteronomy 13:1): ״לא תוסף עליו ולא תגרע ממנו״; and this is what is written (Psalms 19:8): ״תורת יהוה תמימה״. And it was made of hammered work (מקשה) to indicate unity, on account of what is written (Exodus 12:49): ״תורה אחת״. And it was placed in the south, because most wisdom is in the south, and the land of Israel is called the land of the south because it is situated in the choicest of places, in the middle of the climatic zone among the seven climatic zones into which the world is divided, and it is more temperately balanced between cold and heat and is prepared to receive intelligibles. And this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded on (Genesis 2:11–12): ״וזהב הארץ ההיא טוב״ — 'and the gold of that land is good' — there is no Torah like the Torah of the land of Israel, and there is no wisdom like the wisdom of the land of Israel; and furthermore they expounded: 'the air of the land of Israel makes one wise.'" "<b>And observe</b> how the Menorah was placed facing the Table: for the Menorah was in the south and the Table in the north, and these two directions are opposite one another. And this is what is written (Exodus 26:35): ״ושמת את השלחן מחוץ לפרוכת ואת המנורה נכח השלחן״. And the reason is that wisdom cannot endure in a person except through food and drink, for the Table in the north alludes to wealth, as it is written (Job 37:22): ״מצפון זהב יאתה״." "<b>And by way of the Kabbalah,</b> the Menorah alludes to the entirety of emanation. And that it was of hammered work (מקשה) alludes to absolute unity. And the six branches emerging from the central branch correspond to the six that emanate from the central line. And the lamp burning upon them corresponds to Binah, which is above the י', which is ״נר יהוה״ — the lamp of YHWH — from which the soul of man comes. And that it was in the south corresponds to Ḥokhmah; and this is the secret of 'one who wishes to become wise should face south.' And the oil is called יצהר, corresponding to Keter, which is the great luminary; and this is the secret of the anointing oil, with which they would anoint the priests and the kings in the shape of the letter כ״ף, which is an allusion to Keter. And some say in the shape of the letters כ״י, which is an allusion to emanation — the beginning and the end." "And the word מנורת comes in construct state, and there is a difficulty in it, for even though the way of the verses is with such usage, as in ״וזאת תורת זבח השלמים״, it is possible to say here that it comes to explain that it is attached to another Menorah that is its likeness, for the construct is not to the word ״זהב״ alone, since there is no reason of מק״ף there. And so we find Zechariah the prophet, peace be upon him, mentioned it in construct form, for he said (Zechariah 4:2): ״ראיתי והנה מנורת זהב כלה״. And even though he mentioned it in construct form, the reason in it indicates distancing it from the word ״זהב״, to indicate that it is far removed and wondrous beyond the physical thing; and the construct is only to its likeness, for the construct indicates a hidden thing to which it is attached. And the distanced sense from the word ״זהב״ indicates the distance of its likeness from it and from every physical thing, that it cannot be likened or compared to it at all."], :book :exodus, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {3 "The phrase ויש בו טפחא (literally 'there is in it a handbreadth') is rabbinic idiom for 'there is a difficulty in it.' The abbreviation מק״ף has been read as referring to the grammatical category of construct (סמיכות) involving the word זהב; resolved by inference from context."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:04:40.258551699Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}